; Label's line box — where autowrap breaks a line, and how far apart the lines
; that come out of it sit.
;
; The one variable, as a 2x2 matrix over the two inputs that decide it. The
; SAME sentence is set four times, in the SAME `autowrap_mode = 3`, the same
; colour, the same alignment, against a box of one of two widths (320 / 560) at
; one of two font sizes (20 / 28). Reading across a row changes only the box
; width, so only the break points may move; reading down a column changes only
; the font size, so the break points AND the pitch between baselines must both
; move together. Each pane's grey plate is exactly the Label's own rect, so an
; overrun or a wrong first-baseline offset is measurable against the plate edge
; rather than against a guess.
;
; The corner pane is what makes the matrix a matrix: with only one row a
; too-small pitch is indistinguishable from a too-small font, and with only one
; column a wrapper that ignored the box entirely still produces four plausible
; blocks of text.
;
; This is the only golden anywhere that renders shaped, wrapped text. The
; Label3D fixtures are deliberately out of the set, the button golden sets one
; unwrapped word per box, and every remaining 2D scene is untyped geometry — so
; a line-breaking rule that fell back to "never break" (one long line running
; out of every plate) or to breaking mid-word would move nothing else at all.
;
; Measured against Godot 4.6.3 at `--mode 2d` — same break points, same line
; count, same pitch, and each line's right edge within 2 px of the engine's:
;
;   pane           lines  first line top  pitch  line right edges
;   320 / size 20    4        y 55          31   347, 341, 369, 139
;   560 / size 20    2        y 55          31   944, 843
;   320 / size 28    5        y 344          42   353, 374, 297, 348, 259
;   560 / size 28    3        y 344          42   950, 937, 686
;
; Per-line ink is within about 3% of Godot's throughout. The glyphs themselves
; are drawn from an MSDF atlas rather than by the engine's rasteriser, so the
; edge pixels of every stem differ; that is what the whole-frame delta is, and
; it is why this scene's guard is the line box rather than the coverage.

